`idblock.bin` and `id-auth.bin` in this directory are ALL-ZERO placeholders.

They are NOT a real AMD SEV-SNP launch policy. They are signed by NO author
key. A guest launched with these blocks has an UNAUTHENTICATED ID block: the
hypervisor cannot prove the launch was authorised by the image owner.

## Why this file exists — the third belt

PR-F2 used the `measurement_kind` tag ("uki_sha384") as the "not for
production" signal. PR-F3 legitimately reaches `measurement_kind =
"snp_launch_digest_v1"` — the real launch digest — so that belt no longer
flags a dev build.

This marker file IS the replacement belt. While it exists next to an all-zero
`idblock.bin`, the SEV-SNP launch is dev-only:

  1. `measurement_kind` — the §22 ceremony signer still refuses to sign any
     allowlist entry whose kind is not `snp_launch_digest_v1`.
  2. **THIS FILE** — a real production turn-up MUST replace the placeholder
     blocks with a real, signed launch policy and DELETE this marker. A
     reviewer seeing this file deleted without a real IDBLOCK landing
     alongside it is looking at the diff that breaks the production posture.
  3. PR-F4 provenance — the signed provenance map records the IDBLOCK digest,
     so an all-zero block reaching the §22 path is visible out-of-band too.

## Producing a real IDBLOCK

The real IDBLOCK binds the launch policy + the expected measurement and is
signed by an **author key**. Provisioning that key (custody, ceremony) is a
separate future PR — see `README.md`. Do not improvise it here.
